export const DE_C1_S08_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-der-aushilfspfarrer": [
    `I have been the sacristan in Oberhaslach in the Chiemgau for twenty-seven years, and in that time I have seen three priests come and two go, a bishop close up and the Pope on television. I know how much wine goes into a cruet, which candle on the altar is lit first and which altar boy secretly helps himself to the rest of the cruet in the sacristy. Nobody fools me. At least, that's what I believed until that summer.`,
    `Our priest, Father Brandl, seventy-one, had to go to Bad Füssing for eight weeks in June for a cure, because of his spinal discs, and the archdiocese office in Munich, as always, had nobody. Instead came a Herr Haas, a friend of the priest's, so it was said, in his early sixties, with grey hair, a black suit and a voice that made old Frau Obermaier's eyes moist at his very first "Grüß Gott". From the first day he was addressed as "Reverend". He didn't contradict anyone.`,
    `And he preached. Good Lord, how he preached! Not like Father Brandl, who has been giving the same four sermons in rotation for thirty years and loses the thread every time in the second one. Herr Haas spoke about the prodigal son as if he were the father, the son and the fatted calf in one person. After three weeks people came from Traunstein. After five weeks the landlord of the church inn, who hadn't seen the inside of a church since his wedding, was sitting in the third row, and afterwards he said he had imagined it quite differently.`,
    `But I noticed things. Herr Haas never said the Eucharistic prayer. He didn't consecrate. Every Sunday he took the hosts from the tabernacle, where Father Brandl had consecrated them before he left, and when they ran low he had new ones brought by the priest in Traunstein. He wore no stole. And when Frau Obermaier asked him to hear her confession, he said kindly that he wasn't responsible for that. That evening I sat in the sacristy for a long time staring at the unused vestment. A fake priest. In my church.`,
    `On Monday I called the archdiocese. A young woman told me there was no Father or Pater Haas in the entire archdiocese. I thanked her, hung up, and on Tuesday after evening devotions I confronted him in the sacristy, the holy-water bucket in my hand, I don't know why myself. He listened to me, and then he laughed, so heartily that I had to put the bucket down.`,
    `He had never been a priest, he said, and he had never claimed to be. He was a retired grammar-school teacher of German and Latin, a school friend of Brandl's and a trained leader of lay services. What he had been holding every Sunday was not a Mass but a Liturgy of the Word with Holy Communion, which any lay person with the necessary course may lead; it said so in the parish newsletter, by the way. "Have you ever heard me speak the words of consecration, Frau Huber?" I had to admit I hadn't. "You see. The 'Reverend' was your idea. On the first Sunday Frau Obermaier kissed my hand before I could open my mouth. And after that, to be honest, the truth would only have got in the way."`,
    `I slept badly that night. I looked for the parish newsletter, and there it was, on the last page, in small print, under the funerals: "Sundays: Liturgy of the Word with Herr B. Haas". Nobody had read it, me neither. For eight weeks we had had a priest because we wanted one, and Herr Haas had simply not stopped us. That, I felt, was actually the eeriest thing about the whole story.`,
    `In August Father Brandl came back, with a strengthened back and the same old four sermons. After two Sundays the landlord of the church inn complained. Since then Herr Haas has led the Liturgy of the Word on the first Sunday of the month, and it is printed big and bold in the parish newsletter, on the front page. I set it that way myself. On those Sundays the church is fuller than at Easter. Frau Obermaier still kisses his hand and says "Reverend". I have decided that this is none of my business.`,
  ],
  "de-der-waschbaer-und-der-wolf": [
    `They call me a newcomer, and that is the friendliest thing they call me. In the newspapers I am an invasive species, at the farmers' association a pest, and the hunting tenant, Dr Volker Sennhenn, a notary from Frankfurt who climbs the raised hides in our hunting ground on the edge of the Kellerwald at weekends, doesn't call me anything, he shoots. My ancestors were released at the Edersee in the 1930s; people wanted to enrich the local fauna, so it was said. We enriched the fauna. We haven't been welcome since.`,
    `I learned early that in this country you don't survive by strength but by paying attention. At his bait site, a pile of maize at the edge of the forest, the notary had screwed a wildlife camera to an oak, a grey box with a little red light that clicked softly every time something moved in front of it. For weeks I lay under the veranda of his hunting lodge and listened when he looked at the pictures with his guests. So I learned two things: that humans believe what they see on a screen, and that there is nothing they fear as much as the wolf.`,
    `In those days the wolf was only a rumour in our area. People talked about him in the lodge the way you talk about a storm that has struck in another federal state. The notary said that once a wolf showed up in the hunting ground he could forget about hunting; the game would become shy, the guests would stay away, and his wife wouldn't let him out at night any more. I lay under the veranda and understood that I had just heard the most valuable piece of information of my life.`,
    `The rest was craft. I waited for a night in November, with hoarfrost and fog, when the camera's infrared light made everything grey and grainy. From the dog basket at the holiday farm at the edge of the forest I took a tuft of fur from a husky. Then I stepped close to the lens, very close, my fur fluffed up, my head lowered, and let my eyes shine into the little red light. A raccoon standing close enough to a camera is as big as a wolf. That isn't a lie, it's perspective. I put the fur on the maize.`,
    `The following Saturday the notary sat very still in front of his screen. On Monday the state's wolf adviser came, a bearded man who put the fur in a bag and said they would have to wait for the genetic analysis. On Wednesday the local paper ran: "Wolf in the Kellerwald? Hunters worried". The notary gave up his hunting lease before Christmas. I spent a winter of a kind a raccoon otherwise only knows from stories: maize, peace and quiet, and a hunting lodge whose bin nobody locked any more.`,
    `In April, one rainy evening, he stood in front of me. He was young, thin and had a polite calm such as I had never seen in any animal. He came from Lusatia, he said, and had been wandering west for months, always following the stories. "I was told there was already one of us here," he said. "I wanted to meet him." He looked at me for a long time. Then he looked at the oak, at the grey box with the little red light, and I think he understood everything without my having to say a word. "You used my name," he said. It didn't even sound angry. It sounded like someone writing out a bill.`,
    `I moved on that same night, across the main road to the south, where there are gardens and compost heaps and people who think I'm a pest, but at least no wolves. The genetic analysis, I heard later, gave a clear result, by the way: dog. In May the camera then photographed a real wolf. This time nobody believed it.`,
    `Moral: whoever borrows a name must expect the owner to ask for it back one day. And whoever has once cried "wolf" will at some point no longer be believed, not even when it isn't him who is crying.`,
  ],
  "de-doppelt-besetzt": [
    `Uwe Behrendt was fifty-two, a customs officer at Rostock's overseas port and a man nobody turned round to look at. He had always appreciated that. He had a face like a form: functional, completely filled in and forgotten as soon as it was read. That changed in the spring, when television began broadcasting "Baltic Murder", a crime series set in Rostock whose detective chief inspector, a certain Jens Wollin, was played by an actor called Mark Hessler. Mark Hessler had Uwe's face.`,
    `Not a similar one, but exactly his: the same crooked nose, the same heavy eyelids, the same expression of a man who is just wondering whether he turned off the light in the cellar. After the third episode Uwe was asked for an autograph at the baker's. After the fifth a man at the traffic lights called after him, "Well, Wollin, found another body?" His colleagues at the port began calling him "Inspector", and Uwe, who had not once raised his voice in twenty-six years of service, noticed that he didn't mind.`,
    `He watched the series, secretly at first, then with a notepad. Wollin was a taciturn, unhappy man who stood by the city harbour at night staring at the water before he solved a case. Uwe found this exaggerated. Nobody stood by the city harbour at night staring at the water; it was cold and smelled of diesel. Even so, he caught himself ordering his rolls at the baker's with a taciturn, unhappy face, and the shop assistant added a cinnamon pastry for free.`,
    `In June they filmed at the port. Uwe went along in his lunch break, for professional reasons, as he told himself. Mark Hessler sat in a folding chair behind the barrier, smoking, and looked like a man who hadn't been able to go shopping in peace for months. When he saw Uwe he dropped his cigarette. They stood facing each other for a while like two people in front of the same mirror who don't know which of them is the reflection.`,
    `They went for a beer, to a pub at the port that Uwe had frequented for years and where Hessler was greeted at once with "Evening, Uwe". Hessler liked that enormously. He said that since "Baltic Murder" he couldn't take a step without being photographed, and that for a year he had hardly said a sentence that hadn't been written by a scriptwriter. Uwe said that for twenty-six years he hadn't said a sentence that wasn't in a service regulation. Towards midnight, over the fourth beer, Hessler made a suggestion. Uwe thought it was a joke. So did Hessler, until the next morning.`,
    `The following Tuesday Mark Hessler sat in Uwe's uniform at the customs office checking freight documents, and Uwe stood in Hessler's coat by the city harbour and, on the director's instructions, stared at the water. It was cold and smelled of diesel. After the first take the director said he had never been so good, so unaffected, so real. Uwe said nothing, which she took for depth.`,
    `Hessler, for his part, bored by the freight documents, did what Jens Wollin would have done: he became suspicious. A container of supposedly deep-frozen fish seemed too light to him. He had it opened, against a colleague's advice, and found no body in it, but two thousand pairs of counterfeit branded trainers. The head of the customs office shook Uwe's (that is, Hessler's) hand and said that after twenty-six years he would never have credited him with so much instinct.`,
    `Since then they have swapped, irregularly, about once a month. Nobody has ever noticed, either at the customs office or at the TV station, and Uwe doesn't quite know whether that should reassure or unsettle him. The ratings of "Baltic Murder" have gone up, and so have the seizures at the overseas port. Only the shop assistant at the baker's sometimes, when he buys his rolls, puts the cinnamon pastry back in the display, gives him a long look and says: "You're not you today." She is right every time.`,
  ],
  "de-die-krawatte": [
    `In January Henrik Lassen had been transferred from Hanover to Cologne, to the head office of an insurance group, and he had been warned about many things: about Kölsch, which is served in glasses that people in Hanover would take for tasting samples, about colleagues who switch to "du" after two days, and above all about Weiberfastnacht. On that Thursday before Rose Monday, it had been explained to him, the women stormed the offices at eleven minutes past eleven and cut off the men's ties. Henrik, an actuary and by nature a person who calculates risks instead of taking them, considered this an exaggeration. He estimated the probability at under ten per cent.`,
    `Of all days, that Thursday at half past eleven he had his first presentation to the board. As at every important occasion for the past three years, he was wearing his father's tie: dark blue silk with tiny anchors, which his father, a ship's engineer from Cuxhaven, had worn to every christening, every wedding and every funeral. Henrik had not let him take it into the grave. He had thought his father would have said: "Silk is too good for the worms." His father had had a line for everything. Henrik usually didn't.`,
    `At eleven minutes past eleven the door opened. In came a bee, two pirates, a cow and a ladybird with a pair of craft scissors. The ladybird was Carla Nowak from Controlling, with whom Henrik had so far exchanged two sentences, both about spreadsheets. Before he could say anything she had already cut, below the knot, with a clean, expert snip. The bee planted a little kiss on his cheek, the cow blew a party horn, and then they all stood there waiting for him to laugh.`,
    `He didn't laugh. He stood there with the stump under his chin and said quietly, more to himself than to the others: "It was my father's." The bee stopped buzzing. Carla looked at the piece of silk in her hand, at the little anchors, and under the painted black spots her face went completely white. She said: "Oh God." Then she said it again. Then nobody said anything, and from outside someone could be heard shouting "Kölle Alaaf".`,
    `He gave the presentation with the stump. The chairman of the board, a Cologne man from Nippes, looked at him benevolently and said at the end: "Now you've arrived, Herr Lassen." Henrik thanked him and felt as if something had been amputated. At six in the evening, when the building was almost empty, Carla stood at his desk, without antennae and without spots, holding a paper bag. Inside was the tip of the tie. "My grandma was a seamstress in Ehrenfeld," she said. "I learned to sew from her before I could read. Give me the rest. Please."`,
    `He gave her the rest. He didn't know why himself; you don't entrust your only keepsake to a woman who has just cut it up. On Ash Wednesday the tie lay on his desk in tissue paper. The seam was almost invisible. Just there, Carla had embroidered a tiny anchor, a little bigger than the others, and below it, barely legible, in red: 11.11. The tie was now three centimetres shorter. Henrik looked at it for a long time. He thought that his father, who could laugh at anything, would have laughed a great deal at this story, and that he himself had perhaps gone a little too long without laughing.`,
    `In the summer they went for a Kölsch together for the first time, in the autumn for the first time without colleagues. The following January Carla asked whether he wouldn't rather take Weiberfastnacht off, for safety reasons. He said no. That Thursday he came to the office dressed as a sailor, with a cap and a striped shirt, and with a tie he had bought the day before for four euros ninety at the department store, green with yellow ducks.`,
    `At eleven minutes past eleven the ladybird came. Henrik held out the ducks to her. Carla cut, and this time everybody laughed, him too. The other tie, the one with the anchors, he wore that day under his sailor shirt, where nobody could see it. Carla knew anyway. She didn't give him a little kiss on the cheek but somewhere else, and the bee, who had become a bumblebee in the meantime, blew her party horn with enthusiasm.`,
  ],
  "de-friedland-oktober-1955": [
    `On 7 October 1955 Werner's name was in the newspaper, on page three, in a long list in small print, between a Lindemann from Bielefeld and a Lingner from Passau: Lindner, Werner, born 1914, Göttingen. Hanna read the line at breakfast while Karl, beside her, was buttering bread for Peter. She read it three times. Then she folded up the newspaper, as neatly as if that could undo the news, and said: "He's coming." Karl stopped buttering. He put the knife down very gently, the way you put something down in a room where someone is sleeping.`,
    `Eleven years. In the summer of 1944 Werner had been reported missing near Bobruisk. In 1949 a card had come through the Red Cross, twenty-five words, no more were allowed, from a camp whose name Hanna couldn't find in any atlas. After that, nothing. She had waited, as everyone had waited, with the picture on the chest of drawers and the shaving things in the bathroom cabinet. In 1951 she had met Karl, the teacher who gave Peter extra lessons in maths and whose wife had been killed in the bombing in Kassel. He had stayed. They had not married. The neighbours talked about it, and Hanna had never explained to anyone why she hadn't had Werner declared dead, not even to Karl. Karl had never asked.`,
    `On 9 October they drove to Friedland. Karl drove, Hanna sat beside him, Peter stayed with the neighbour; he was thirteen and knew his father only from a photo in which a man in uniform held a baby as if it were fragile. The camp lay in a shallow valley, huts with corrugated-iron roofs, and on the square in front of it thousands were crowding. Women held up photos stuck on cardboard, with questions underneath: "Who knows my son? Last seen Stalingrad." A man was selling sausages. It was drizzling. Karl stayed with the car. "I'll wait here," he said. "This isn't my place." Hanna wanted to object and didn't.`,
    `Around noon the buses came from Herleshausen. The bell above the camp began to ring, and somewhere at the front someone began to sing "Now Thank We All Our God", and the crowd joined in, hesitantly at first, then with a force that frightened Hanna. The men who got off the buses wore padded jackets and fur hats and all looked the same, grey, thin and old, no matter how old they were. Hanna thought: I won't recognise him. Eleven years, and I won't recognise him.`,
    `She recognised him by the way he stood: his weight on his left leg, his right hand in his jacket pocket, as if he were looking for a key in it. That was how he had stood at the station in 1938, when she first saw him. She walked towards him. He looked at her, and for a second, a terrible second, there was nothing in his face. Then he said: "Hanna." Not like a question. Like something you have learned by heart for a very long time.`,
    `They stood facing each other, and neither knew what to do with their hands. Finally he took her hand, formally, as in a dancing lesson. He asked about Peter. She said Peter had grown, he played football, he was good at maths. Werner nodded. Then he looked past her, across the square, to the car park, where a man in a grey coat stood next to a car, smoking and not looking over, not looking over so intently that you could see it from far away. Werner looked for a long time. "You have someone," he said. It was not an accusation. Hanna said nothing, and that was the answer.`,
    `"Is he good to the boy?" asked Werner. "Yes," said Hanna. Werner nodded again, slowly, like a man checking a bill and finding that it is correct, even if he doesn't like the total. Then he said he wanted to see the boy. Today. For eleven years he had thought of hardly anything but the face in the photo, and he wanted to know what had become of it. Everything else, he said, they would see.`,
    `They walked to the car park together. As they came closer, Karl stubbed out his cigarette, took off his hat and, without a word, opened the back door. Werner looked at him, then got in. Hanna sat in the front. On the drive to Göttingen nobody spoke. Once, at a crossroads, Hanna looked in the rear-view mirror and saw two men both looking straight ahead at the road, as if what lay in front of them was the only thing anyone needed to think about at the moment. Perhaps, she thought, it was.`,
  ],
};

